<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhabru edict</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Calcutta-Bairat edict</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhabru edict, also called the Calcutta-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bairat">Bairat</a> edict, is an inscription of the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> found near Bairat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and now kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>. Addressed to the Buddhist monastic community, it declares Ashoka's faith in the Buddha, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> and the Sangha and recommends seven texts for monks, nuns and lay followers to study. It is one of the clearest statements of his personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p></div>
